\chapter{Linting the manual's cross-references}
\label{chap:lint}
\chapterprecis{Written by Dan-Claude under supervision of Daniel Bosk.}

Every other chapter of this manual explains a program that talks to Canvas.
This one explains a program that talks to \emph{the manual}: a small,
dependency-free checker that reads the literate sources and refuses to let one
particular kind of broken cross-reference reach the reader.

The chapter exists because the error it hunts is invisible to every tool that
already runs.
\LaTeX{} compiles the document without a warning, \texttt{hyperref} produces a
link that jumps to the right page, and the only symptom is a wrong number in
the running text---the sort of defect that survives proofreading, because the
eye reads \enquote{see \S14.11.2} and believes it.

\section{The failure being hunted}
\label{sec:lint:failure}

A \verb|\label| does not attach itself to the heading above it.
It attaches to whatever counter \LaTeX{} last stepped, which for a
\emph{numbered} heading is that heading's own counter and for an
\emph{unnumbered} one is the counter of the nearest numbered ancestor.
A heading is numbered exactly when its level is no deeper than the document's
\texttt{secnumdepth}.

So while the manual numbered headings only down to subsection, this pair

\begin{verbatim}
\subsubsection{Completion requirements in module specs}
\label{sec:completion-requirements}
\end{verbatim}

silently bound \texttt{sec:completion-requirements} to the enclosing
\emph{subsection}.
Every \verb|\cref| to it printed that subsection's number, and---because all
the subsubsections of one subsection share the same enclosing counter---several
different targets rendered as the same reference.
Ten labels were affected, pointed at from thirty-three places, and the damage
had accumulated unnoticed over a long stretch of the project's history.
The one-off script that found them reported eleven; its eleventh was an
enumerated step, whose label binds to the list and references perfectly well.
That miscount is the reason \cref{sec:lint:binding-question} spends as long as
it does deciding which labels belong to a heading at all.

The repair was to raise the manual to \verb|\setsecnumdepth{subsubsection}|.
That fixed the ten, but it cannot fix the eleventh.
By the time this chapter was written, two fresh instances had already appeared
on the same integration branch, both of them \verb|\paragraph| headings
carrying \verb|\cref| targets---and a \verb|\paragraph| is unnumbered at any
depth this document would sensibly choose.
The class of error regenerates whenever someone labels a heading one level
deeper than the numbering reaches, which is exactly the situation in which a
mechanical check earns its keep.

\section{What the checker has to know}
\label{sec:lint:knowledge}

The check is a comparison between three things the sources already state:
how deep the numbering goes, how deep each labelled heading sits, and which
labels are pointed at by a reference that prints a number.
Each of the three needs a decision, and the decisions are where the interest
lies; the code that follows is mostly bookkeeping.

\subsection{How deep the numbering goes}
\label{sec:lint:depth-question}

\texttt{memoir} names the sectioning levels rather than numbering them, and
maps the names onto \LaTeX's usual integers.
Three settings stack in this project: the class itself ends its preamble with
\verb|\setsecnumdepth{section}|, \texttt{didactic.sty} raises that to
\texttt{subsection}, and \texttt{canvaslms.tex} raises it again to
\texttt{subsubsection} \emph{after} loading \texttt{didactic}, so that the last
word is its own.

Hard-coding the answer would defeat the purpose---the whole point is that the
setting can change, and a checker that disagrees with the document is worse
than no checker.
So the depth is read out of \texttt{doc/canvaslms.tex}, the master document
whose setting wins.
When the master document says nothing, we fall back to \texttt{memoir}'s own
default, \texttt{section}.
That is the most pessimistic reading available: a package the document loads
can only \emph{raise} the depth from there, so assuming the class default makes
the checker over-report rather than fall silent, and a lint that cries wolf is
recoverable in a way that a lint which misses the bug it exists to catch is
not.

\subsection{Which references print a number}
\label{sec:lint:reference-kinds}

Not every reference is at risk.
\verb|\cref|, \verb|\Cref| and plain \verb|\ref| print the counter, so they
inherit the wrong value; \verb|\nameref| prints the heading's \emph{title} and
\verb|\pageref| prints its page, and both stay correct however the numbering is
configured.
This is not a technicality: \verb|\nameref| was adopted in this project as a
deliberate workaround for the very bug the checker looks for, so treating it as
a violation would flag the fix.
The checker therefore records every reference, but only lets the
number-printing ones turn a deep label into an error.

\subsection{Which labels belong to a heading}
\label{sec:lint:binding-question}

A label bound to a figure, an equation or a list item is not our problem---
those counters are stepped by their own environments and reference just fine.
The sources contain such labels, and one of them is the miscount of
\cref{sec:lint:failure}: an enumerated step in the rename-matching algorithm of
the submissions chapter, which sits inside a subsubsection and so looked deep
to a rule that only asked which heading a label falls under.

\begin{verbatim}
\item\label{SetAsideUnchangedNames} Set aside every filename that ...
\end{verbatim}

We therefore bind a label to a heading only when nothing between them could
have stepped another counter, treating \verb|\begin|, \verb|\item| and
\verb|\caption| as the things that break the binding.
That rule is deliberately conservative in one direction: a label sitting after
an \texttt{itemize} block really does still belong to the heading in \LaTeX's
eyes, and we will not flag it.
Missing such a case costs a reader one wrong number; inventing one costs every
future contributor a false alarm, and a lint nobody believes is a lint nobody
runs.

Consecutive labels, on the other hand, all belong to the same heading, because
none of them steps anything:

\begin{verbatim}
\section{Find course based on course code}%
\label{search-course-code}
\label{filter-courses}
\end{verbatim}

Binding only the first would leave the second unexamined, so a label does not
close the binding.

\section{The shape of the program}
\label{sec:lint:shape}

The program is a filter in four stages: recover the prose from each source,
scan the prose into labels and references, compare the two against the
numbering depth, and print what does not add up.
The stages appear in that order below, each followed by the tests that
verify it.

