\section{The effective numbering depth}
\label{sec:lint:secnumdepth}

We start with the depth, because it is the number every later judgement is made
against.
\texttt{memoir}'s name-to-level table is the one the class uses internally,
extended with the three pseudo-names it also accepts; keeping the two in one
expression records that the depth names are exactly the sectioning names plus
\texttt{none}, \texttt{book} and \texttt{all}.

<<constants>>=
HEADING_LEVELS = {
    "part": -1,
    "chapter": 0,
    "section": 1,
    "subsection": 2,
    "subsubsection": 3,
    "paragraph": 4,
    "subparagraph": 5,
}
SECNUMDEPTH_LEVELS = dict(HEADING_LEVELS, none=-10, book=-2, all=50)
MEMOIR_DEFAULT_SECNUMDEPTH = HEADING_LEVELS["section"]
MASTER_DOCUMENT = "doc/canvaslms.tex"
@

Both spellings of the setting have to be recognised.
\verb|\setsecnumdepth| is \texttt{memoir}'s interface and the one this project
uses, but \verb|\setcounter| naming the counter directly is what a document
inherited from another class would say, and a checker that ignored it would
report the class default while the document did something else entirely.

<<imports>>=
import re
@

<<constants>>=
SECNUMDEPTH_RE = re.compile(
    r"\\setsecnumdepth\s*\{\s*(?P<name>[a-z]+)\s*\}"
    r"|\\setcounter\s*\{\s*secnumdepth\s*\}\s*\{\s*(?P<number>-?\d+)\s*\}"
)
@

The last setting in the file wins, mirroring \LaTeX's own behaviour: this is
precisely why \texttt{canvaslms.tex} places its \verb|\setsecnumdepth| after
\verb|\usepackage{didactic}|, and a checker that took the first match would
read the value the document deliberately overrode.

<<functions>>=
def read_secnumdepth(path):
    """Return the numbering depth set by the document at `path`.

    Returns None when the document sets no depth, or does not exist.
    """
    if not path.exists():
        return None
    depth = None
    for line in path.read_text().splitlines():
        for match in SECNUMDEPTH_RE.finditer(strip_comment(line)):
            if match.group("name") is not None:
                depth = SECNUMDEPTH_LEVELS.get(match.group("name"), depth)
            else:
                depth = int(match.group("number"))
    return depth
@

The depth also has to be expressible on the command line, where a name is
kinder to type than the integer it stands for, while the integer must stay
available for a document that sets the counter directly.

<<functions>>=
def parse_depth(text):
    """Return the numbering depth named or numbered by `text`."""
    if text in SECNUMDEPTH_LEVELS:
        return SECNUMDEPTH_LEVELS[text]
    try:
        return int(text)
    except ValueError:
        raise ValueError(f"unknown numbering depth: {text}") from None


def depth_name(depth):
    """Return the sectioning name for `depth`, or its number as text."""
    for name, level in SECNUMDEPTH_LEVELS.items():
        if level == depth:
            return name
    return str(depth)
@

Let us verify that the depth is read the way the manual's own preamble is
written---the package's setting first, the document's own setting after it, and
the latter winning.

<<test helpers>>=
def write(tmp_path, text, name="chapter.nw"):
  """Write `text` to a source file under `tmp_path` and return its path."""
  path = tmp_path / name
  path.write_text(text)
  return path
@

<<test functions>>=
def test_the_last_depth_setting_wins(tmp_path):
  """The document's own setting overrides the one the package made."""
  path = write(tmp_path, r"""
\usepackage{didactic}
\setsecnumdepth{subsection}
\setsecnumdepth{subsubsection}
""", name="master.tex")
  assert read_secnumdepth(path) == 3

def test_setcounter_spelling_is_understood(tmp_path):
  """A document inheriting the raw counter must not be misread."""
  path = write(tmp_path, r"\setcounter{secnumdepth}{2}", name="master.tex")
  assert read_secnumdepth(path) == 2

def test_a_commented_out_setting_is_ignored(tmp_path):
  """A setting behind a percent sign has no effect on the document."""
  path = write(tmp_path, "%\\setsecnumdepth{paragraph}\n", name="master.tex")
  assert read_secnumdepth(path) is None

def test_a_document_without_a_setting_reports_nothing(tmp_path):
  """Absence is reported as None so the caller can apply its fallback."""
  path = write(tmp_path, r"\documentclass{memoir}", name="master.tex")
  assert read_secnumdepth(path) is None

def test_depths_can_be_named_or_numbered():
  """Both spellings reach the same integer."""
  assert parse_depth("subsubsection") == parse_depth("3") == 3
@

\section{Recovering the prose from a literate source}
\label{sec:lint:prose}

A \texttt{.nw} file is not \LaTeX{}; it is \LaTeX{} interleaved with code, and
the code is full of text that looks like markup but is not.
Feeding the raw file to the scanner would produce nonsense, so each source is
first reduced to the lines \texttt{noweave} would actually pass through to the
document.
Blank lines take the place of everything removed, so that every finding still
carries the line number a reader can jump to.

<<functions>>=
def documentation_lines(text, *, noweb):
    """Return the LaTeX prose of `text`, one entry per source line."""
    lines = text.splitlines()
    if noweb:
        lines = without_quoted_code(without_code_chunks(lines))
    return without_comments(without_verbatim(lines))
@

\subsection{Code chunks}
\label{sec:lint:code-chunks}

The most convincing argument for discarding code chunks is in the quizzes
chapter (\cref{quizzes-command}), where the exporter \emph{writes} a manual of
its own:

\begin{verbatim}
output_buffer.append(f"\\subsection{{{title}}}\\label{{{label}}}\n\n")
\end{verbatim}

That line defines no heading in this document, and a checker that believed it
would report a label the reader can never see.
Chunks are recognised the way \texttt{noweb} recognises them: a definition line
opens one, and a line whose first character is \texttt{@}, followed by space or
end of line, returns to documentation---carrying whatever else that line holds.

The two patterns below have to write \texttt{noweb}'s own markup inside a
\texttt{noweb} code chunk, so the opening angle brackets are escaped with
\texttt{@} to keep \texttt{notangle} from reading them as a chunk reference.

<<constants>>=
CHUNK_START_RE = re.compile(r"^@<<.*>>=\s*$")
CHUNK_END_RE = re.compile(r"^@(\s|$)")
@

<<functions>>=
def without_code_chunks(lines):
    """Blank the code chunks of a noweb source, keeping line numbers."""
    prose = []
    in_code = False
    for line in lines:
        if CHUNK_START_RE.match(line):
            in_code = True
            prose.append("")
        elif in_code and CHUNK_END_RE.match(line):
            in_code = False
            prose.append(line[1:])
        elif in_code:
            prose.append("")
        else:
            prose.append(line)
    return prose
@

\subsection{Verbatim examples}
\label{sec:lint:verbatim}

This chapter is its own best argument for discarding verbatim blocks too.
\Cref{sec:lint:failure} displays a broken heading-and-label pair in order to
explain the bug; without this step the checker would dutifully report its own
illustration, and every attempt to document a cross-reference convention would
trip the tool meant to enforce it.

<<constants>>=
VERBATIM_ENVIRONMENTS = ("verbatim", "Verbatim", "minted", "lstlisting")
VERBATIM_BEGIN_RE = re.compile(
    r"\\begin\{(" + "|".join(VERBATIM_ENVIRONMENTS) + r")\*?\}"
)
@

<<functions>>=
def without_verbatim(lines):
    """Blank verbatim-like environments, keeping line numbers."""
    prose = []
    environment = None
    for line in lines:
        if environment is None:
            begun = VERBATIM_BEGIN_RE.search(line)
            environment = begun.group(1) if begun else None
            prose.append("" if begun else line)
        else:
            if re.search(r"\\end\{" + environment + r"\*?\}", line):
                environment = None
            prose.append("")
    return prose
@

\subsection{Quoted code and comments}
\label{sec:lint:quotes}

Two smaller reductions remain.
\texttt{noweb}'s double-bracket quotes hold code, not markup, so prose that
mentions a macro by quoting it must not be mistaken for a use of it---this
chapter's own paragraphs would otherwise register as references.
\LaTeX{} comments are dropped for the reason a compiler drops them: a
commented-out reference points at nothing, and a commented-out heading numbers
nothing.

The comment rule respects the escaped percent sign, but not the rarer case of a
literal backslash immediately before a real comment.
Telling those apart needs a character-by-character walk over the escapes, and
no source in this project writes the second.

<<constants>>=
QUOTED_CODE_RE = re.compile(r"\[\[.*?\]\]")
COMMENT_RE = re.compile(r"(?<!\\)%.*$")
@

<<functions>>=
def without_quoted_code(lines):
    """Blank noweb's code quotes, which are not LaTeX markup."""
    return [QUOTED_CODE_RE.sub("", line) for line in lines]


def strip_comment(line):
    """Return `line` without its trailing LaTeX comment."""
    return COMMENT_RE.sub("", line)


def without_comments(lines):
    """Return `lines` with every LaTeX comment removed."""
    return [strip_comment(line) for line in lines]
@

Let us verify that each of the four reductions hides what it should.
Building the chunk delimiters in a helper keeps the escaping of
\cref{sec:lint:code-chunks} in one place instead of in every test that needs a
code chunk.

<<test helpers>>=
def chunk(name, *lines, ending="@"):
  """Return a noweb code chunk called `name` with `lines` as its body."""
  return "\n".join([f"@<<{name}>>=", *lines, ending, ""])
@

<<test functions>>=
def test_a_heading_inside_a_code_chunk_is_invisible():
  """LaTeX that a program prints is not LaTeX this document contains."""
  text = chunk("[[export.py]]",
               'buffer.append("\\\\subsection{X}\\\\label{sec:x}")')
  assert documentation_lines(text, noweb=True) == ["", "", ""]

def test_prose_after_the_at_sign_survives():
  """A documentation chunk may start on the same line as its @."""
  text = chunk("[[a.py]]", "pass", ending="@ \\label{sec:kept}")
  assert "\\label{sec:kept}" in documentation_lines(text, noweb=True)[2]

def test_a_verbatim_example_is_invisible():
  """A displayed example must not be read as markup."""
  text = "\\begin{verbatim}\n\\subsubsection{X}\n\\label{sec:x}\n" \
         "\\end{verbatim}\n"
  assert documentation_lines(text, noweb=True) == ["", "", "", ""]

def test_a_quoted_macro_is_not_a_use_of_it():
  """Prose that quotes a reference does not thereby make one."""
  text = "We write [[\\cref{sec:x}]] to point at it.\n"
  assert "cref" not in documentation_lines(text, noweb=True)[0]

def test_a_commented_reference_is_invisible():
  """A commented-out reference points at nothing."""
  assert documentation_lines("% \\cref{sec:x}\n", noweb=True) == [""]
@

\section{Scanning for headings, labels and references}
\label{sec:lint:scan}

What the scanner produces is two lists: the labels, each remembering the
heading it belongs to, and the references, each remembering the macro that made
it.
Recording the macro rather than a boolean keeps the finding messages able to
name what they saw, and lets \cref{sec:lint:reference-kinds}'s distinction stay
a property of the data instead of a decision taken at scan time.

<<imports>>=
import dataclasses
import pathlib
@

<<data structures>>=
@dataclasses.dataclass(frozen=True)
class Heading:
    """A sectioning command and the line it was found on."""

    kind: str
    line: int

    @property
    def level(self):
        """The LaTeX sectioning level of this heading."""
        return HEADING_LEVELS[self.kind]


@dataclasses.dataclass(frozen=True)
class Label:
    """A label, and the heading it binds to when it binds to one."""

    name: str
    path: pathlib.Path
    line: int
    heading: Heading | None


@dataclasses.dataclass(frozen=True)
class Reference:
    """One use of one label by one referencing macro."""

    name: str
    path: pathlib.Path
    line: int
    macro: str

    @property
    def prints_a_number(self):
        """Whether this macro renders the target's counter."""
        return self.macro in NUMBERED_REFERENCE_MACROS
@

The referencing macros are grouped by what they print rather than by which
package defines them, since that is the only distinction the check cares about.
The range forms of \texttt{cleveref} are separated out again because they take
two arguments, and reading only the first would leave the far end of every
range unchecked.

<<constants>>=
NUMBERED_REFERENCE_MACROS = (
    "cref",
    "Cref",
    "crefrange",
    "Crefrange",
    "labelcref",
    "autoref",
    "eqref",
    "ref",
)
SILENT_REFERENCE_MACROS = ("nameref", "Nameref", "pageref")
RANGE_REFERENCE_MACROS = ("crefrange", "Crefrange")
SINGLE_REFERENCE_MACROS = tuple(
    macro
    for macro in NUMBERED_REFERENCE_MACROS + SILENT_REFERENCE_MACROS
    if macro not in RANGE_REFERENCE_MACROS
)
BINDING_BREAKERS = ("begin", "item", "caption")
@

All of it is recognised by a single expression, because the order in which the
tokens appear on a line is what decides the binding: a label before a
\verb|\begin| on the same line belongs to the heading above, one after it does
not.
Scanning with separate patterns would throw that ordering away.

The alternatives are emitted longest first, so \verb|\subsubsection| is not
consumed by the shorter \verb|\subsection| branch and \verb|\crefrange| reaches
its two-argument branch instead of matching \verb|\cref| and failing.
Sorting them rather than writing them out in a careful order means the
constants above can stay grouped by meaning.

<<pattern helpers>>=
def alternatives(names):
    """Return a regex alternation of `names`, longest first."""
    return "|".join(sorted(names, key=len, reverse=True))
@

<<constants>>=
TOKEN_RE = re.compile(
    r"\\(?P<heading>" + alternatives(HEADING_LEVELS) + r")\*?\s*(?=[\[{])"
    r"|\\label\s*\{(?P<label>[^}]*)\}"
    r"|\\(?P<range_macro>" + alternatives(RANGE_REFERENCE_MACROS) + r")\*?"
    r"\s*\{(?P<first>[^}]*)\}\s*\{(?P<last>[^}]*)\}"
    r"|\\(?P<macro>" + alternatives(SINGLE_REFERENCE_MACROS) + r")\*?"
    r"\s*\{(?P<targets>[^}]*)\}"
    r"|\\(?P<breaker>" + alternatives(BINDING_BREAKERS) + r")\b"
)
@

The scan itself is the binding rule of \cref{sec:lint:binding-question} written
out: a heading arms the binding, a breaker disarms it, and a label does
neither, so consecutive labels all bind to the same heading.

<<functions>>=
def scan(text, path):
    """Return the (labels, references) of a LaTeX or noweb source.

    `text` is the file's content and `path` is the name to report it by.
    """
    labels, references = [], []
    heading = None
    prose = documentation_lines(text, noweb=path.suffix == ".nw")
    for number, line in enumerate(prose, 1):
        for token in TOKEN_RE.finditer(line):
            <<record what the token is>>
    return labels, references
@

<<record what the token is>>=
if token.group("heading"):
    heading = Heading(token.group("heading"), number)
elif token.group("label") is not None:
    labels.append(Label(token.group("label").strip(), path, number, heading))
elif token.group("breaker"):
    heading = None
else:
    <<record a reference to every target named>>
@

A \verb|\cref| may name several targets at once, and a range names its two
endpoints; flattening both into one reference per target lets everything
downstream work with a single, simple kind of thing.

<<record a reference to every target named>>=
macro = token.group("macro") or token.group("range_macro")
targets = token.group("targets")
if targets is None:
    targets = f"{token.group('first')},{token.group('last')}"
for target in targets.split(","):
    if target.strip():
        references.append(Reference(target.strip(), path, number, macro))
@

Now let us verify the binding rule against the three shapes
\cref{sec:lint:binding-question} discussed: the ordinary one, the
consecutive-labels one that a first-match-wins rule would half-miss, and the
list item that must not be mistaken for a heading label at all.

<<test helpers>>=
def scan_text(text, name="chapter.nw"):
  """Scan `text` as though it were a source file called `name`."""
  return scan(text, pathlib.Path(name))
@

<<test functions>>=
def test_a_label_binds_to_the_heading_above_it():
  labels, _ = scan_text("\\subsection{Title}\n\\label{sec:x}\n")
  assert labels[0].heading.kind == "subsection"
  assert labels[0].heading.level == 2

def test_consecutive_labels_all_bind_to_the_same_heading():
  """Both labels on a heading are at risk, so both must be examined."""
  labels, _ = scan_text(
    "\\section{Title}%\n\\label{search}\n\\label{filter}\n"
  )
  assert [label.name for label in labels] == ["search", "filter"]
  assert all(label.heading.kind == "section" for label in labels)

def test_a_list_item_label_is_not_a_heading_label():
  """An enumerated step has a counter of its own and references correctly."""
  labels, _ = scan_text(
    "\\subsection{Title}\n\\begin{enumerate}\n\\item\\label{step} Do it\n"
  )
  assert labels[0].heading is None

def test_every_target_of_a_cref_is_recorded():
  _, references = scan_text("See \\cref{sec:a,sec:b}.\n")
  assert [reference.name for reference in references] == ["sec:a", "sec:b"]

def test_both_ends_of_a_range_are_recorded():
  _, references = scan_text("See \\crefrange{sec:a}{sec:b}.\n")
  assert [reference.name for reference in references] == ["sec:a", "sec:b"]

def test_a_nameref_is_recorded_but_prints_no_number():
  _, references = scan_text("See \\nameref{sec:a}.\n")
  assert not references[0].prints_a_number
@

\section{The audit}
\label{sec:lint:audit}

A finding is deliberately one line of text with a file and a line number in
front of it, the shape every editor and every \texttt{grep} pipeline already
knows how to follow.

<<data structures>>=
@dataclasses.dataclass(frozen=True)
class Finding:
    """Something the audit wants a human to look at."""

    severity: str
    path: pathlib.Path
    line: int
    message: str

    def __str__(self):
        return f"{self.path}:{self.line}: {self.severity}: {self.message}"
@

The audit reads every source once, indexes the references by the name they
point at, and then asks three questions of the result.

<<imports>>=
import collections
@

<<functions>>=
def audit(paths, *, secnumdepth, report_unreferenced=False, root=None):
    """Return the findings of auditing `paths` at depth `secnumdepth`.

    `root` is the directory that findings are reported relative to.
    """
    labels, references = [], []
    for path in paths:
        found_labels, found_references = scan(
            path.read_text(), display_path(path, root)
        )
        labels.extend(found_labels)
        references.extend(found_references)
    <<index the references by the label they name>>
    findings = []
    <<flag referenced headings that carry no number>>
    <<flag references to labels that do not exist>>
    <<flag labels that nothing refers to>>
    return sorted(findings, key=lambda f: (str(f.path), f.line, f.message))


def display_path(path, root):
    """Return `path` relative to `root` when it lies below it."""
    if root is None:
        return path
    try:
        return path.relative_to(root)
    except ValueError:
        return path
@

Two indexes are needed rather than one, because the two questions differ:
whether a label is \emph{at risk} depends only on the number-printing uses,
while whether it is used at all counts every kind.

<<index the references by the label they name>>=
numbered_uses = collections.defaultdict(list)
every_use = collections.defaultdict(list)
for reference in references:
    every_use[reference.name].append(reference)
    if reference.prints_a_number:
        numbered_uses[reference.name].append(reference)
@

The central question is the one from \cref{sec:lint:failure}.
A deep label nobody points at is harmless---it prints nothing---so the error is
reserved for the combination, and the message names the sites so that the
reader can judge the damage without running a search of their own.

<<flag referenced headings that carry no number>>=
for label in labels:
    if label.heading is None or label.heading.level <= secnumdepth:
        continue
    uses = numbered_uses[label.name]
    if not uses:
        continue
    sites = ", ".join(f"{use.path}:{use.line}" for use in uses)
    findings.append(
        Finding(
            "error",
            label.path,
            label.line,
            f"label {label.name!r} is on a \\{label.heading.kind}, which is "
            f"unnumbered at secnumdepth {secnumdepth} "
            f"({depth_name(secnumdepth)}); "
            f"{len(uses)} reference(s) print the enclosing number: {sites}",
        )
    )
@

A reference to a label no source defines is a different fault with the same
cause---usually a label renamed while one of its uses was missed---and \LaTeX{}
reports it only as a question mark in the output.
Since the audit has already collected every label in the tree, catching it
costs one comparison.

<<flag references to labels that do not exist>>=
defined = {label.name for label in labels}
for reference in references:
    if reference.name not in defined:
        findings.append(
            Finding(
                "error",
                reference.path,
                reference.line,
                f"reference to undefined label {reference.name!r}",
            )
        )
@

The third question is the one the original audit answered with a long list of
harmless leftovers.
An unreferenced label is not an error---it may be a deliberate anchor for a
chapter not yet written---so it is reported only when asked for, and never
affects the exit status.

<<flag labels that nothing refers to>>=
if report_unreferenced:
    for label in labels:
        if not every_use[label.name]:
            findings.append(
                Finding(
                    "info",
                    label.path,
                    label.line,
                    f"label {label.name!r} is never referenced",
                )
            )
@

Now let us verify the audit, and in particular that one and the same source is
judged differently at different depths---which is the whole reason the depth is
read from the document instead of assumed.

<<test helpers>>=
def audit_text(tmp_path, text, *, secnumdepth=3, **kwargs):
  """Audit `text` as a single source file and return the findings."""
  return audit([write(tmp_path, text)], secnumdepth=secnumdepth, **kwargs)

def errors(findings):
  """Return only the findings that make the lint fail."""
  return [finding for finding in findings if finding.severity == "error"]
@

<<test functions>>=
REFERENCED_SUBSUBSECTION = """
\\section{Enclosing}
\\label{sec:enclosing}
\\subsubsection{Deep}
\\label{sec:deep}
See \\cref{sec:deep} and \\cref{sec:enclosing}.
"""

def test_a_referenced_subsubsection_is_fine_when_numbered(tmp_path):
  """At subsubsection depth the label has a counter of its own."""
  findings = audit_text(tmp_path, REFERENCED_SUBSUBSECTION, secnumdepth=3)
  assert errors(findings) == []

def test_the_same_source_breaks_at_subsection_depth(tmp_path):
  """The failure the manual used to have, reproduced by lowering the depth."""
  findings = audit_text(tmp_path, REFERENCED_SUBSUBSECTION, secnumdepth=2)
  assert len(errors(findings)) == 1
  assert "sec:deep" in errors(findings)[0].message

def test_a_referenced_paragraph_is_an_error(tmp_path):
  """A paragraph is unnumbered at every depth this manual would choose."""
  findings = audit_text(tmp_path, """
\\subsection{Enclosing}
\\paragraph{Deep}
\\label{sec:deep}
See \\cref{sec:deep}.
""")
  assert len(errors(findings)) == 1
  assert "\\paragraph" in errors(findings)[0].message

def test_a_nameref_to_a_deep_label_is_accepted(tmp_path):
  """The sanctioned workaround must not be reported as the bug."""
  findings = audit_text(tmp_path, """
\\subsection{Enclosing}
\\paragraph{Deep}
\\label{sec:deep}
See \\nameref{sec:deep}.
""")
  assert errors(findings) == []

def test_an_unreferenced_deep_label_is_not_an_error(tmp_path):
  """A label nobody points at prints nothing, so it breaks nothing."""
  findings = audit_text(tmp_path, """
\\subsection{Enclosing}
\\paragraph{Deep}
\\label{sec:deep}
""")
  assert errors(findings) == []

def test_an_unreferenced_label_is_reported_on_request(tmp_path):
  findings = audit_text(tmp_path, """
\\section{Alone}
\\label{sec:alone}
""", report_unreferenced=True)
  assert [finding.severity for finding in findings] == ["info"]

def test_a_reference_to_nothing_is_an_error(tmp_path):
  findings = audit_text(tmp_path, "See \\cref{sec:typo}.\n")
  assert len(errors(findings)) == 1
  assert "undefined" in errors(findings)[0].message
