\section{Choosing what to audit}
\label{sec:lint:files}

Left to itself the script audits the whole repository, because the bug is a
property of the document as a whole: a label in one chapter is referenced from
another, and a check that saw only one file could not tell a deep label from an
undefined one.

The patterns name the hand-written sources.
\texttt{src} is searched recursively because the package is a tree; the other
two are not, which is what keeps the \texttt{didactic} submodule under
\texttt{doc} out of the audit---its sources belong to another project and are
not part of this document.

<<constants>>=
SOURCE_PATTERNS = ("src/**/*.nw", "tests/*.nw", "doc/*.nw", "doc/*.tex")
@

One filter is still needed.
\texttt{doc} holds both hand-written \texttt{.tex} files and the ones
\texttt{noweave} produces from the \texttt{.nw} sources beside them, and
auditing both would report every label in those chapters as its own duplicate,
while pointing the findings at a generated file nobody can edit.
A \texttt{.tex} with a \texttt{.nw} of the same name is therefore taken to be
that file's weave.

<<functions>>=
def is_woven(path):
    """Return whether `path` was woven from a noweb source beside it."""
    return path.suffix == ".tex" and path.with_suffix(".nw").exists()


def source_files(root):
    """Return the manual's hand-written sources below `root`."""
    paths = []
    for pattern in SOURCE_PATTERNS:
        paths.extend(sorted(root.glob(pattern)))
    return [path for path in paths if not is_woven(path)]
@

<<test functions>>=
def test_a_woven_tex_file_is_not_audited(tmp_path):
  """Auditing a chapter and its weave would duplicate every label."""
  (tmp_path / "doc").mkdir()
  (tmp_path / "doc" / "intro.nw").write_text("")
  (tmp_path / "doc" / "intro.tex").write_text("")
  (tmp_path / "doc" / "preamble.tex").write_text("")
  found = [path.name for path in source_files(tmp_path)]
  assert found == ["intro.nw", "preamble.tex"]
@

\section{The command line}
\label{sec:lint:cli}

The interface is small: where the repository is, how deep the numbering goes,
and whether to mention unused labels.
The depth is an option rather than a constant so that the checker can be
pointed at an older revision of the sources, where the document was configured
differently---which is how the eleven labels of \cref{sec:lint:failure} can
still be demonstrated today.

<<imports>>=
import argparse
import sys
@

<<constants>>=
DEFAULT_ROOT = pathlib.Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent
@

<<functions>>=
def parse_arguments(argv=None):
    """Return the parsed command line."""
    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(
        description="Check that cross-referenced headings are numbered."
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "files",
        nargs="*",
        type=pathlib.Path,
        help="sources to audit (default: every source in the repository)",
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "-r",
        "--root",
        type=pathlib.Path,
        default=DEFAULT_ROOT,
        help="repository root (default: the one this script lives in)",
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "-d",
        "--secnumdepth",
        help="numbering depth, named or numbered "
        f"(default: as set in {MASTER_DOCUMENT})",
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "-u",
        "--unreferenced",
        action="store_true",
        help="also report labels that nothing references",
    )
    return parser.parse_args(argv)
@

What remains is the assembly: settle the depth, choose the files, audit them,
print the result.

<<functions>>=
def main(argv=None):
    """Audit the manual; return 0 when it is clean and 1 when it is not."""
    options = parse_arguments(argv)
    <<settle the numbering depth>>
    paths = options.files or source_files(options.root)
    findings = audit(
        paths,
        secnumdepth=secnumdepth,
        report_unreferenced=options.unreferenced,
        root=options.root,
    )
    <<print the findings and choose the exit status>>
@

The fallback of \cref{sec:lint:depth-question} is announced rather than applied
silently, because a checker running against a document it could not read is
exactly the situation in which a clean report means least.

<<settle the numbering depth>>=
if options.secnumdepth is not None:
    secnumdepth = parse_depth(options.secnumdepth)
else:
    secnumdepth = read_secnumdepth(options.root / MASTER_DOCUMENT)
    if secnumdepth is None:
        print(
            f"warning: no numbering depth found in {MASTER_DOCUMENT}; "
            f"assuming memoir's default, "
            f"{depth_name(MEMOIR_DEFAULT_SECNUMDEPTH)}",
            file=sys.stderr,
        )
        secnumdepth = MEMOIR_DEFAULT_SECNUMDEPTH
@

Findings go to standard output so that they can be piped, and the verdict to
standard error so that piping the findings does not swallow it.
Only errors change the exit status; the informational findings are advice.

<<print the findings and choose the exit status>>=
for finding in findings:
    print(finding)
failures = [finding for finding in findings if finding.severity == "error"]
if failures:
    print(f"{len(failures)} broken cross-reference(s)", file=sys.stderr)
    return 1
return 0
@

\section{Guarding the manual from the test suite}
\label{sec:lint:regression}

The checker is wired in twice.
\texttt{make lint} in \texttt{doc} runs it directly, which is what a
contributor reaches for while editing; and the test below runs the same audit
over this repository as part of the ordinary \texttt{pytest} run, which is what
actually stops a regression, since that is the check nobody has to remember to
invoke.

The test reads the depth from the master document rather than naming a number,
so that raising or lowering the numbering stays a one-line change in
\texttt{canvaslms.tex}---the test follows the document instead of contradicting
it.

<<test functions>>=
def test_the_manual_has_no_broken_cross_references():
  """Every referenced heading in this repository is numbered."""
  depth = read_secnumdepth(DEFAULT_ROOT / MASTER_DOCUMENT)
  assert depth is not None, f"no numbering depth in {MASTER_DOCUMENT}"
  findings = audit(
    source_files(DEFAULT_ROOT), secnumdepth=depth, root=DEFAULT_ROOT
  )
  assert errors(findings) == [], "\n".join(
    str(finding) for finding in errors(findings)
  )
@

\section{What this check does not catch}
\label{sec:lint:limitations}

Three limitations are worth stating, all of them consequences of choices made
earlier.

\begin{description}
\item[Labels behind an environment] A label separated from its heading by a
  \verb|\begin| is left alone, per \cref{sec:lint:binding-question}.  In
  \LaTeX{} it may still belong to the heading; here it is assumed to belong to
  something with a counter of its own.
\item[Duplicate labels] Two labels with the same name are a genuine fault, but
  one \LaTeX{} does warn about, so the checker leaves it to the build.
\item[Labels defined outside the sources] A reference to a label that a package
  defines would be reported as undefined.  No such reference exists in this
  manual, and adding one would be unusual enough to deserve the reminder.
\end{description}
